      "UV-C air purifiers get marketed hard on the promise of killing mold spores and bacteria outright, and it's worth setting expectations before you buy one: air moves through a purifier's UV-C chamber in a fraction of a second, which is nowhere near the exposure time UV-C needs to reliably inactivate a mold spore's tough outer wall. So we tested the AC5900WCA on its merits as a HEPA purifier first, UV-C second. On that front, it performs well — the true HEPA filter met its 99.97%-at-0.3-micron rating in our particle testing, and the 743 sq ft coverage rating held up in a room that size, bringing spore-simulant counts down at a comparable rate to pricier competitors. The charcoal filter handles light, everyday odors adequately but saturates faster than we'd like against a genuinely musty room — plan on replacing it every 3-4 months rather than the label's suggested 6-8 in a mold-affected space. The UV-C bulb is a legitimate bonus against surface-level bacteria and viruses that pass close to the lamp, and it doesn't hurt anything by being there, but don't buy this unit believing UV-C is doing the heavy lifting against mold. At its price point, it's a genuinely capable HEPA purifier with a nice-to-have extra.",
