        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Mold Spores Matter -->
        <div id="why-mold-spores" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Mold Spores Are a Real Air Quality Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            Mold reproduces by releasing spores — microscopic reproductive particles typically between 2 and 10 microns in size — into the surrounding air. A single colony of visible mold can release millions of spores per day, and those spores don't stay put. They travel through HVAC ducts, drift under doors, and settle throughout a home, landing anywhere humidity and organic material give them a chance to establish a new colony. This is why mold problems that start in a basement or bathroom often show up as allergy-like symptoms — sneezing, congestion, throat irritation, headaches — in rooms far from the original source.
          </p>

