            4. Placement & How to Run an Air Purifier for Mold Correctly
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buying the right unit only gets you halfway there. Placement and operation determine whether it actually reduces spore counts or just moves air around a room. Here's how to get it right:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Place It Close to the Source, Not the Corner</h3>
          <p>
            The instinct to tuck a purifier into an unused corner works against you. Position it as close as practical to the suspected mold source — near a damp wall, behind a washing machine, or beside a bathroom vent — so it captures the highest concentration of spores before they disperse across the room. Keep at least a foot of clearance on all sides for proper airflow, and avoid placing it directly against a wall or under furniture.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Run It Continuously, Not Intermittently</h3>
          <p>
            Mold spores are constantly released wherever there's an active moisture source, so running a purifier for a few hours a day lets counts rebuild in between. For a genuine mold concern, plan to run the unit 24/7 on auto mode (or a moderate fixed speed) rather than switching it on only when you're in the room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Close the Room Off From the Rest of the House</h3>
          <p>
            If mold is concentrated in one room, keep the door closed while the purifier runs so you're not diluting its effort across the whole house — and so you're not pulling spore-laden air into hallways and bedrooms. Once counts have dropped and stabilized, you can widen coverage or add a second smaller unit elsewhere.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Replace Filters on a Mold-Specific Schedule</h3>
          <p>
            Manufacturer filter-life estimates assume typical household dust, not an active mold source. In a musty basement or bathroom, plan to replace activated carbon filters roughly twice as often as the label suggests, and inspect HEPA filters monthly for visible growth or heavy staining — a saturated filter isn't just less effective, it can become a secondary spore source itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Pair It With Source Control</h3>
          <p>
            An air purifier reduces exposure while you address the underlying moisture — it isn't a substitute for fixing a leak, improving ventilation, or removing visible mold growth. Treat it as one part of a broader plan, not the whole solution.
          </p>
