          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Air Purifier vs. Dehumidifier for Mold: You Probably Need Both
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the single most common point of confusion for people shopping for "mold air purifiers," and it matters: <strong>an air purifier and a dehumidifier solve two completely different problems.</strong>
          </p>

          <p>
            A <strong>dehumidifier</strong> addresses the root cause. Mold needs relative humidity above roughly 60% to establish and spread; keeping a room consistently below 50% starves new colonies before they can take hold. If you have visible mold growth or a chronically damp space — a basement, crawlspace, or bathroom with poor ventilation — a dehumidifier is the tool that actually stops the problem from getting worse.
          </p>

          <p>
            An <strong>air purifier</strong> addresses the symptom. It removes spores that are already airborne, reducing what you breathe and helping control the musty smell, but it does nothing to lower humidity or stop existing mold from spreading across a surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you can only buy one and you have <strong>active, visible mold growth</strong>, buy the dehumidifier first — an air purifier running next to a wall of active mold is treating symptoms while the underlying problem continues to worsen. If your mold has already been professionally remediated and you're managing residual spores and odor during recovery, an air purifier is the more immediately useful tool. For anyone dealing with a genuinely mold-prone space long-term, running both together is the combination that actually keeps counts down.
          </p>
        </div>

